Let Local signing take executables with chained fixups and __init_offsets.

build_signed_container.py refused every executable with LC_DYLD_CHAINED_FIXUPS ("Tolkara's
loader applies classic dyld info only"), and found initializers only in __mod_init_func.
Tolkara's loader applies chained fixups (GuestFixups.c), and a page container holds only
__TEXT: what matters is that no fixup lands in __TEXT, since its pages are mapped read and
execute from the signed container and cannot be written. The chained fixups' per-segment
starts say exactly that: __TEXT must have none, or only pages that start no chain. The
first initializer is taken from __init_offsets (32-bit offsets from the header, which
starts __TEXT) when there is no __mod_init_func, as the runtime's loader does; with chained
fixups a __mod_init_func slot holds a chain entry rather than an address, so that pairing is
refused.
Brighter Shores (chained fixups, five initializers in __init_offsets, no fixup in __TEXT)
then runs with Local signing: no tunnel, no Developer service.

The container is signed with the developer identity by codesign(1), which Xcode's own builds
have already made able to use the signing key, instead of tools/sign_guest_local, whose key
access macOS approves per build of the tool (a keychain prompt after every rebuild); with
several identities matching, the one whose signature names the team is kept. The result is
still checked by sign_guest_local --verify. Upstream candidate.

